WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.910
Part ten: the review. Every figure in Anil's return, where it came from - and anything he types over it, marked as his.

2
00:00:10.531 --> 00:00:15.811
Salary from both Form 16s, each with its source beside it.

3
00:00:16.421 --> 00:00:25.611
His residential status, from the days he was in India. A resident: all his income counts, wherever it arose.

4
00:00:26.233 --> 00:00:44.493
Arrears for 2022-23 were paid to him this year. That year's income and regime give the relief under section 89, at that year's own slabs - and for a year over fifty lakh, that year's surcharge too - with a reminder to file Form 10 E.

5
00:00:45.125 --> 00:01:07.394
Income and assets outside India have their own cards. Here, his father's account in the U S that he can sign for: Schedule F A, table D - trusts abroad and other income from outside India have their tables too. And his minor son's interest is added to his income, less the exemption for each child.

6
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:18.129
A flat he owns half of, with his wife, let out: rent, municipal tax and loan interest, each taken by his fifty percent share.

7
00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:25.113
Online-game winnings and a crypto sale: taxed at thirty percent, whatever his slab.

8
00:01:25.750 --> 00:01:32.060
Self-assessment tax he paid himself, with the B S R code and challan number.

9
00:01:32.671 --> 00:01:46.181
Capital gains: the four sales read from the C A M S P D F - one grandfathered to January 2018 - and a plot he typed, with the buyer's P A N from the 26 A S.

10
00:01:46.827 --> 00:01:58.567
The plot was bought in 2010. Twelve and a half percent without indexation, or twenty percent with it: SimplyFile works out both and takes the cheaper.

11
00:01:59.186 --> 00:02:17.446
Two exemptions on the one sale: twenty lakh in 54 E C bonds, and a new house under 54 F - each taken in turn against the gain still left. And each is checked against its time limit: the bonds within six months, the house within two years.

12
00:02:18.061 --> 00:02:31.961
Losses brought forward: one click fills them from last year's I T R 3 - a short-term loss from 2024-25 and last year's own long-term loss - set off against this year's gains.

13
00:02:32.593 --> 00:02:42.933
Two gifts: two lakh from an uncle - a relative, so it is free - and seventy-five thousand from a friend: over fifty thousand in the year, so it is income.

14
00:02:43.531 --> 00:02:52.841
Deductions from Form 16 - and more for the old regime, 80 D D to 80 G G C, with what each schedule asks for.

15
00:02:53.468 --> 00:03:04.048
A few questions last: his contact details, and his share of farm income - forty thousand. It is exempt, but it is shown in the return.

16
00:03:04.671 --> 00:03:09.591
File: with capital gains, his form is I T R 2.

17
00:03:10.186 --> 00:03:21.686
The department's JSON, checked against its own schema - valid. He downloads it and uploads it on the e-filing portal: SimplyFile never files for him.

18
00:03:22.296 --> 00:03:26.016
Once filed, he notes the acknowledgement.

19
00:03:26.639 --> 00:03:50.329
And for 2024-25 he left out two lakh of short-term gains: the updated return, I T R U. The gains at that year's own fifteen percent, the interest - 234 C for each instalment among it - and the additional tax, all worked out. A year over fifty lakh gets its surcharge, with marginal relief.

20
00:03:50.952 --> 00:03:57.792
Next, part eleven: the same screen for a CA firm's families and consultants.
